<!--
  Authoritative source: the client certificate Apigee itself received during
  the two-way TLS handshake on its own VirtualHost, when mutual auth
  terminates at the gateway. tls.client.raw.cert/.s.dn/.i.dn are only
  populated when the VirtualHost has ClientAuthEnabled + a TrustStore
  configured (Edge Private/Public Cloud) or
  propagateTLSInformation.clientProperties = true (Apigee X/hybrid); that is
  environment/VirtualHost config outside this bundle, not something a
  SharedFlowBundle can set.

  Only reached (see sharedflows/default.xml's Condition) when
  tls.client.raw.cert is confirmed non-null, so it is safe to use <Template>
  here (which, unlike <Ref>, always produces a string, even ""; running it
  unconditionally would make client.cert.base64.step1 resolve to "" instead
  of staying null when TLS gave us nothing, which would wrongly skip the
  header fallback further down the flow).

  client.cert.base64.step1 is the first of 3 chained steps that strip
  tls.client.raw.cert's PEM armor and internal line-wrap whitespace down to
  bare base64 (see AM-Extract-Client-Cert-Strip-Step2/Final for the rest,
  and this bundle's root descriptor for why they're 3 SEPARATE policies
  rather than 3 AssignVariable blocks in one). replaceAll's regex is
  Apigee's own message-template function (Java regex under the hood; not
  JavaScript/Java code; no JavaCallout needed for this).

  Whitespace comes out FIRST, before the BEGIN/END literal removal, not
  after: stripping \s collapses the whole certificate onto one line, which
  also removes the single space between "BEGIN"/"END" and "CERTIFICATE" (a
  space is whitespace too), so the PEM armor's BEGIN CERTIFICATE marker line
  collapses down to one contiguous word, one literal to remove instead of
  needing "BEGIN", "END", and "CERTIFICATE" as three separate patterns. Doing
  the whitespace strip last would still work, but doing it first is simpler
  and is one fewer policy.

  client.cert.subject.dn/.issuer.dn/.subject.dn.base64/.issuer.dn.base64
  don't have this chaining problem (each is a single, independent
  AssignVariable reading directly from a real pre-existing flow variable,
  tls.client.s.dn/tls.client.i.dn), so they stay in this one policy.
-->
<AssignMessage name="AM-Extract-Client-Cert-TLS">
    <DisplayName>AM Extract Client Cert TLS</DisplayName>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.base64.step1</Name>
        <Template>{replaceAll(tls.client.raw.cert,'\\s','')}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.subject.dn</Name>
        <Ref>tls.client.s.dn</Ref>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.issuer.dn</Name>
        <Ref>tls.client.i.dn</Ref>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.subject.dn.base64</Name>
        <Template>{encodeBase64(tls.client.s.dn)}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.issuer.dn.base64</Name>
        <Template>{encodeBase64(tls.client.i.dn)}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</AssignMessage>
